# 5.4 Growth of Complexity

One reason the Travelling Salesman Problem is so important is its rapid increase in computational complexity.

The number of possible tours for

\[
n
\]

cities is

\[
\frac{(n-1)!}{2}
\]

for symmetric TSP.

Table 5.1 demonstrates the explosive growth.

| Number of Cities | Possible Tours |
|------------------|---------------:|
| 4 | 3 |
| 5 | 12 |
| 6 | 60 |
| 8 | 2,520 |
| 10 | 181,440 |
| 15 | 43,589,145,600 |
| 20 | 60,822,550,204,416,000 |

As the number of cities increases, evaluating every possible route rapidly becomes computationally impossible.

This phenomenon is known as **combinatorial explosion**.

---

# 5.5 Why TSP is NP-Hard

The Travelling Salesman Problem belongs to the class of **NP-hard problems**.

Unlike polynomial-time problems,

there is currently no known algorithm capable of solving every TSP instance efficiently.

For small numbers of cities,

optimal solutions can be obtained relatively easily.

However,

for realistic industrial problems involving hundreds or thousands of locations,

the computational cost becomes enormous.

This explains why companies frequently rely on approximation algorithms rather than exact solutions.

Quantum optimization research aims to improve this situation by exploring new computational paradigms.

